  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers in the east exit by early in the evening; \nfollowed by a few showers and increased winds this evening in the \nwest.\n\nA surface cold front remains positioned to the east of the area. An \narea of low pressure riding northeast along this front has helped to \nstall it, and allow for showers over eastern parts of the area this \nafternoon. As the the low continues on its journey northeast along \nthe front, the showers will come to an end from west to east. Where \ncloud cover in the east is still abundant, it too will be decreasing \nin coverage from west to east.\n\nBehind the cold front, this system's parent upper trough is still \nprogression through the Lower Ohio Valley. This feature is expected \nto reach, and then cross the area overnight. With it will come \nincreased gusty winds, and colder temperatures. Dew points have \nalready starting falling across much of the area, and that will only \naccelerate overnight. The increase in northwest winds will yield \nupslope cloud cover from the mountains of North Carolina north into \nsoutheast West Virginia. Additionally over southeast West Virginia, \nsome isolated to scattered showers are expected through the late \nevening before ending.\n\nWinds through the overnight hours are expected to gust to the 20 to \n30 mph range over the mountains, with some higher gusts nearing 35 \nmph. Over the Piedmont, gusts of 10 to 20 mph will be common. \nSimilar winds speed will continue through mid-day Saturday, before \nstarting to subside during the late afternoon into the evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry and trending milder through the middle of next \nweek.\n\nOur weather pattern is expected to remain one dominated by generally \nnorthwest flow aloft through the the middle portion of next week. \nWhile a series of low pressure systems are expected to cross the \nGreat Lakes region into New England, the moisture and energy \nassociated with them is expected to remain to the north. Our coolest \ntemperatures are expected on Sunday with values near normal for this \ntime of year. As we progress into mid-week, geopotential heights are \nexpected to gradually rises, and so too the surface temperatures. By \nWednesday and Thursday, temperatures are expected to be five to ten \ndegrees above normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cooler temperatures and a return of precipitation to \nconclude the week.\n\nWhile specific timing of features still is in question, the general \ntrend among most model guidance is for a longwave trough to dig into \nthe Mississippi Valley on Thursday and progress east towards or into \nour region by Friday. The result will be a trend towards lower \ntemperatures and better chance of precipitation for the area.",
